---
import site from '../data/site.json';
---
<section id="background">
  <h2>Background</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    Menstruation in space is currently managed mainly by hormonal suppression.
    On long missions that depends on drug storage, mission length that may
    change, and in some cases refrigeration. Tampons and pads create waste,
    whilst commercial menstrual cups rely on gravity.
  </p>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num">~{site.flight.reduced_gravity_s} s</div>
      <div class="label">of reduced gravity per parabola, the test window on each flight</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="num">{site.bench.target_ml} mL</div>
      <div class="label">containment target, from 2–12 mL collected over 8 hours</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>
    A menstrual cup is reusable and holds fluid instead of absorbing it, but
    in microgravity surface tension and adhesion dominate how the fluid
    behaves, so a conventional open cup cannot be relied on to keep it inside
    during removal. The SpaceCup adds a closing mechanism that seals the
    opening first, operable one-handed with two fingers.
  </p>
  <p>
    The work set out to answer three questions: which sealing mechanism,
    material and geometry give the best usability, manufacturability and
    anatomical fit; whether it contains fluid under reduced gravity; and how
    it can be cleaned where water is scarce. It builds on a base cup defined
    in a 2025 bachelor thesis of Simone Pfeiffer at the same group (wall
    {site.base_cup.wall_mm} mm, rim outer diameter {site.base_cup.rim_od_mm} mm,
    length {site.base_cup.length_mm} mm without stem, capacity {site.base_cup.capacity_ml} mL).
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .stat-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
    margin: 24px 0 28px;
  }
  .stat {
    padding: 20px 22px;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .stat .num {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 32px;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .stat .label {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
    line-height: 1.45;
    max-width: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .stat-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
